A||||7||GT Bastard||anybody sell grand am brake kits?||||1||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||01-31-2000||11:58 PM||||I've seen how this is done before, but does anybody make a kit that's ready-2-go?||||reg||1
Z||000001||JetroGT||02-01-2000||05:36 AM||||I don't know if these kits are from a Grand AM. But I think the cheaper kits use some GM parts, while retaining your parking brake.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/index.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/index.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||02-01-2000||12:38 PM||||Friends,<P>A guentleman (Jr.) sells them.  He is located in FL.  His page can be found here: <A HREF="http://www.jisod.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.jisod.com</A>   Remember that you will not have an emergency brake after doing the rear brake conversion unless you figure a solution out on your own.<P>I have never met this gentleman and therefore am not vouching for his integrity or product quality.  I simply know that he sells them. <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000004||Monkeyman||02-01-2000||04:18 PM||||Is this just for the front or are the brakes the same on the back?  Anyone know?||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||02-01-2000||05:26 PM||||Monkey,<P>You are correct... the kit is for "front only".  I have never paid that much attention to the page.  Thank you for correcting me.  The cars that I have seen with the GA upgrade all have done the rear or adjusted the proportioning valve.  If only the front brake system is changed and all else remains the same, the fronts will lock up and the rears will not be near the sticktion threshold.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000006||theogre||02-01-2000||07:32 PM||||Doing only the front brakes is very acceptable. The front's do most of the work. You MUST use an adjustable proportioning valve if you do only the front. <P>While adjusting the valve is a bit of a pain, the end result is the rear of the car stays stock and keeps the E-brake. You won't have to worry about remembering what parts fit the back.<P>If you look at other 4 wheel disk cars you'll find it's normal for the rears to be far weaker than the front.<P>an adjustable valve can also help some handling issues when tire sizes are changed. changing tires will affect braking. using wider tires in rear than front will affect brake balance. The adjustable valve alows you to compensate for these changes.||||reg||1
Z||000007||daved||02-01-2000||07:43 PM||||I looked at JR's site, and right at the bottom he mentions that GrandAm brakes are bolt-on for the rear, so you dont need a kit, and of course we lose the parking brake (Big deal...all new stuff on my '86GT Auto...first sign of cold weather and the handbrake locked on and burnt my rotor! we can all do with replacement rears, or do without)  What we need is someone to provide a kit, reasonably priced, to adapt a separate mechanical caliper for the rear handbrake...anyone listening?||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||02-01-2000||11:10 PM||||Having personally done a complete Grand Am brake swap, I can tell you the back brakes are not 100% bolt on. It's easy to do, but you have to grind a little off the caliper bracket in two places and trim a little piece off the dust shield. I used a hand-held angle-head 4 inch grinder which worked great. <P>Ogre is right on the money with his proportioning valve lecture.<P>Anybody else see the FOCOA 3rd quarter 1999 magazine article about the "Big Brakes For 84-87 Fieros"? They apparently have a brake adaptor kit to mount the bigger 10.5 inch rotors and calipers for $430 plus $140 core charge for the hubs. This kit uses 86 IROC rear calipers, so you retain the use of your e-brake. Looks like the same setup that Ryane Motor Sports is selling for twice that amount. I'm half tempted to try them on my Batmobile. I can always swap the Grand Am brakes over to my 85 GT.||||reg||1
